From: Ahrion Gallegos <ahrionmgallegos@gmail.com>
Subject: bus: mhi: host: optional doorbell write on every queued buffer

Adds force_db_brst_disable, an MHI core module parameter that turns off
doorbell burst mode for every channel configured to use it, so the host
writes the channel doorbell register on every queued buffer instead of
only when the device asks.

Default off. With it off the driver behaves exactly as it does without
this patch, which is the point: it exists to A/B test a downlink stall on
one flash rather than two, and to be switched back off in place if it
turns out not to be the cause.

The reasoning and the measurements behind it are in the comment above the
parameter, next to the only code that reads it, so they stay with the
change. Briefly: on this board nothing on the host ever writes the
downlink data channel's doorbell except an M3-to-M0 power transition,
because the device only re-arms db_mode when it runs out of descriptors
and the host is careful never to let that happen. Under sustained traffic
there are no power transitions, so there are no doorbell writes at all.

The parameter is read while channels are configured, which happens when
the controller is registered -- not when the value changes. So re-probe
the modem to apply it:

  echo 1 > /sys/module/mhi/parameters/force_db_brst_disable
  echo 0000:01:00.0 > /sys/bus/pci/drivers/mhi-pci-generic/unbind
  echo 0000:01:00.0 > /sys/bus/pci/drivers/mhi-pci-generic/bind

Confirm it took with

  dmesg | grep 'forcing doorbell writes'

which should name channels 100 and 101. Write 0 and re-probe again to go
back, so both directions are testable without reflashing.

Note that a reboot does not keep the setting: the module reloads with the
default. Making it persistent means passing it at module load time, which
is deliberately left out of this patch -- nothing should be made permanent
before the test says it helps.

Cost when enabled is one MMIO write per queued buffer.

Out-of-tree patch for the GL-X3000 lean build; independent of 991 and
992 and can be dropped on its own.

diff -uprN a/drivers/bus/mhi/host/init.c b/drivers/bus/mhi/host/init.c
--- a/drivers/bus/mhi/host/init.c
+++ b/drivers/bus/mhi/host/init.c
@@ -772,6 +772,44 @@ error_ev_cfg:
 	return -EINVAL;
 }
 
+/* Burst mode (MHI_DB_BRST_ENABLE) lets the device fetch transfer descriptors
+ * without the host writing the doorbell register: mhi_db_brstmode() writes it
+ * only while db_mode is set, and clears db_mode afterwards. The device re-arms
+ * it by sending MHI_EV_CC_DB_MODE when it wants poking again.
+ *
+ * On the GL-X3000's RM520N-GL that re-arm does not arrive on the downlink data
+ * channel. Measured across nine minutes of continuous traffic: the uplink
+ * doorbell tracked its write pointer exactly, while the downlink doorbell did
+ * not move once -- and then began stepping only in lockstep with M3-to-M0
+ * transitions, which re-arm db_mode for an unrelated reason
+ * (mhi_pm_m0_transition() honours doorbell_mode_switch).
+ *
+ * The asymmetry has a cause. The device drains the uplink ring to empty, so it
+ * keeps asking for a doorbell and keeps getting one. The host deliberately
+ * never lets the downlink ring run dry -- mhi_mbim_dl_callback() refills once
+ * free descriptors reach half the queue -- so the device never has to ask, and
+ * never does. The very thing keeping the downlink well fed leaves it with no
+ * way to be woken.
+ *
+ * Under sustained traffic there are no power transitions, so nothing writes the
+ * downlink doorbell at all. If the device stops fetching and waits for one, the
+ * link stalls until traffic drops long enough for a suspend/resume cycle, or
+ * until something forces a wake.
+ *
+ * Setting this forces MHI_DB_BRST_DISABLE on every channel configured for burst
+ * mode, so the doorbell is written on every queued buffer. The cost is one MMIO
+ * write per buffer. Read while channels are configured, so a change takes
+ * effect at the next controller registration -- a modem re-probe or a reboot,
+ * not immediately.
+ *
+ * Default off: with it off the driver behaves exactly as it does without this
+ * patch, which keeps it usable as an A/B test rather than a behaviour change.
+ */
+static bool force_db_brst_disable;
+module_param(force_db_brst_disable, bool, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(force_db_brst_disable,
+		 "Write the channel doorbell on every queued buffer rather than only when the device asks for it (default 0)");
+
 static int parse_ch_cfg(struct mhi_controller *mhi_cntrl,
 			const struct mhi_controller_config *config)
 {
@@ -871,6 +909,13 @@ static int parse_ch_cfg(struct mhi_contr
 			}
 		}
 
+		if (force_db_brst_disable &&
+		    mhi_chan->db_cfg.brstmode == MHI_DB_BRST_ENABLE) {
+			mhi_chan->db_cfg.brstmode = MHI_DB_BRST_DISABLE;
+			dev_info(dev, "ch%u %s: forcing doorbell writes\n",
+				 mhi_chan->chan, mhi_chan->name);
+		}
+
 		if (mhi_chan->db_cfg.brstmode == MHI_DB_BRST_ENABLE)
 			mhi_chan->db_cfg.process_db = mhi_db_brstmode;
 		else
